module runtime.abi_os

/// Port of `runtime/src/os.rs`, the OS-facing half: process termination.
///
/// WHY A SEPARATE MODULE. `runtime.os` holds the *model* of the `os.rs`
/// contract (`exitCodeOf`, `snapshotStrings`): pure Pickle, unit-tested, and
/// deliberately free of any `#[symbol]` slot so `os_test` keeps exercising the
/// native bodies. This file owns the one slot that can be filled without the
/// object model and without the collector - `ExitProcess` is an ordinary Win32
/// import reached through `win_exit`, the same way `win_thread_id` reaches
/// `GetCurrentThreadId`.
///
/// WHY IT IS NOT IN `abi_exception`. That module's own header records the
/// measured rule: a `#[symbol]` fn is linked only when Pickle code actually
/// *calls* it, so defining an entry point is not activation, and swapping one
/// channel in underneath a native one corrupts output silently rather than
/// failing. Process exit is not part of the exception channel, so it must not
/// be promoted alongside it - hence a module of its own, activated on its own.
///
/// THE FLUSH HALF OF THE REFERENCE. `os.rs::pickle_exit` flushes and then
/// terminates. There is nothing to flush here by construction: the ported
/// console writes with `WriteFile` on every call, and even the native
/// `console.rs::write_to_con` does `write_all` *then* `flush` for each write,
/// so no byte is ever left buffered and `pickle_flush` is redundant on that
/// path - which is why `abi_console` can port it as an exact no-op. Only the
/// termination half needs to exist for a runtime-free build.
///
/// THE CLAMP. `os.rs` clamps to the `i32` range before exiting, and the
/// freestanding arm then hands the value to `ExitProcess` as a `UINT`. The
/// clamp is what makes the two agree for negative codes: `std::process::exit`
/// casts the same clamped `i32`, so `-1` reaches the process status block as
/// `0xFFFFFFFF` either way. `runtime.os.exitCodeOf` is that clamp, ported and
/// unit-tested, so the body hands off to it rather than re-deriving the
/// bounds.
import { exitCodeOf } from runtime.os

/// ABI: terminate the process with `code`.
///
/// Returns nothing rather than being typed `-> !`: the language has no never
/// type, the single emitter call site (`emit.rs`, the `exit(code)` builtin) is
/// an `extern_call_void` that discards the result, and the call is
/// unreachable-after anyway because `ExitProcess` does not return.
#[symbol("pickle_exit")]
fn abiExit(code: int) {
    win_exit(exitCodeOf(code))
}
